<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="chap-lebesgue-measure" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Lebesgue Measure</title>
<introduction>

<blockquote>
<p><em><q>It may well be suspected that, had it not been for its prestigious name, [Riemann integration] would have been dropped long ago, for (with due reverence to Riemann’s genius) it is certainly quite clear to any working mathematician that nowadays such a <q>theory</q> has at best the importance of a mildly interesting exercise in the general theory of measure and integration.</q></em></p>
<attribution>
Jean Dieudonné
</attribution>
</blockquote>

<p>
    In this chapter we will study measures on the real line. 
    We begin with the most important example in <xref ref="sec-Lebesgue"/>: the Lebesgue measure.
    We will show several important properties of the Lebesgue measure.
    Amongst them, we will show that all Borel sets are Lebesgue measureable.
    In <xref ref="sec-nonmeasurable"/> we show that there are Lebesgue measurable sets which are not Borel sets, and we will show that there are subsets of the real line which are not Lebesgue measurable.
    In the final section, <xref ref="sec-borel-on-R"/>, we study other Borel measures on the real line, giving a correspondence between certain increasing functions and Borel measures.
</p>
</introduction>

